  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="global_energy_security::operating_margin_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in drone attack frequency causes direct physical damage and temporary shutdowns at oil, gas and power facilities and raises immediate repair and security operating costs, reducing operating margins for affected energy firms.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_security</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="monetary_currency_shift::exchange_rate_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">An uptick in cross‑border drone attacks raises operational and political risk for the countries directly involved, triggering capital outflows and risk premia that depress the affected countries' currencies versus safe‑haven currencies.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">monetary_currency_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="strait_of_hormuz::naval_presence_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in drone attacks in the Russia–Ukraine theatre accelerates diffusion of commercially available strike drones and operational tactics to regional proxies and black‑market suppliers, which in turn leads Gulf states and extra‑regional navies to surge patrols and escorts in the Strait to deter and interdict similar attacks.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_of_hormuz</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in high-volume commercial/weaponized drone attacks demonstrably reliant on dual‑use components (IMUs, navigation chips, RF modules) prompts US/EU policymakers to broaden and tighten export controls on those components to deny easy transfer to strategic competitors (including PRC-linked supply chains).</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::ai_dc_investment_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising drone attack frequency creates immediate procurement demand for AI-based detection, tracking and command-and-control systems and associated cloud/edge compute, accelerating corporate and government investment into AI R&amp;D and data‑center capacity to host those capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-12 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
