<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" target="global_energy_security::investor_sentiment_index" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Announcements or tightening of export controls suddenly increase regulatory and market-access risk for publicly listed tech firms, prompting immediate de-risking and lower valuations from investors.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_security</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" target="monetary_currency_shift::currency_intervention_fl" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sharper export controls create episodic trade- and payments-disruptions and sudden re-pricing of exposure for corporates and banks, which raises short-term FX volatility and prompts more frequent unilateral or coordinated central-bank market operations to stabilize exchange rates.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-12 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">monetary_currency_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" target="strait_of_hormuz::naval_presence_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">A sudden tightening of export controls by the U.S. (or allied packages) provokes Beijing to prioritize secure sea-lines for energy and trade, prompting more frequent Chinese naval escorts, patrols, and combined exercises through/near the Strait of Hormuz as a deterrent and reassurance measure.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_of_hormuz</data>
</edge>
